    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d44">

                <pb facs="0058" n="50" sameAs="frus:frus1938v02/pg50"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.4131/1738: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-08-15T13:00:00+01:00">August 15, 1938—1
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 15—10:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>764. Embassy’s 758, August 13, 1 p.m.<anchor n="49" xml:id="d44-f1"/> This
                    morning Brown told us that the Department’s latest proposals on lumber gave him
                    grounds for hope that a solution of this question was possible. If the
                    Scandinavian countries, particularly Sweden could be made to accept the
                    Department’s contention that this proposal would increase the exports of their
                    lumber he saw no reason for not agreeing to that proposal. He had not yet taken
                    the matter up with the Scandinavian countries but is planning to sound them out
                    in the near future.</p>
                <pb facs="0059" n="51" xml:id="pg51"/>
                <p>He stated that Swedes having got wind of the earlier United States proposition
                    outlined in the Department’s 395, July 27, 7 p.m., approached him on the
                    question. After some discussion the Swedish Government categorically refused to
                    accept the position taken by the Department. He said that the Finns likewise
                    were aware of the Department’s proposal but did not indicate that the matter had
                    been discussed with them. It is assumed from what he said that the Swedish
                    Government, and perhaps the Finnish Government, is aware of the British position
                    on most-favored-nation treatment on imports of soft lumber including Douglas
                    fir.</p>
                <p>In view of my telephone conversation yesterday with Hickerson<anchor n="50"
                        xml:id="d44-f2"/> we did not approach Brown on the basis of the Department’s
                    444, August 12, 7 p.m.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
